correspond to equal off-axis angles. The projection polynomial predicts
relative radius from off-axis angle:
\deqn{\rho = \frac{r}{r_{norm}} = f(\theta') =
  \sum_{k=1}^{p} a_k (\theta')^k}
Here, \eqn{r_{norm}} is \code{normalization_radius}, and the coefficients
\eqn{a_1, \ldots, a_p} are the \code{projection_coef} vector supplied through
\code{parameters}. Its length sets the polynomial degree. The normalization
reference satisfies \eqn{f(\theta'_{norm}) = 1}, where
\eqn{\theta'_{norm}} is \code{normalization_angle} expressed in radians.
See \code{\link[=normalize_projection_coef]{normalize_projection_coef()}} to change this reference and the
corresponding radius in pixels, including for the standard projections.

To recover off-axis angle at a pixel, invert this polynomial on
\verb{[0, max_off_axis_angle]} (degrees), where it must be strictly increasing.
\code{\link[=calc_relative_radius]{calc_relative_radius()}} and \code{\link[=calc_off_axis_angle]{calc_off_axis_angle()}} evaluate the same
model in either direction for numeric vectors.
Relative radii above one are allowed within this interval. Internal azimuth
follows directly from the image offsets:
\deqn{\alpha = \operatorname{atan2}(dx, -dy_{down}) \bmod 2\pi}
}

\subsection{Anisotropic projection}{

